<section>
    <h2>One errand, five floors</h2>
    <p>
        The Warden tested floors 2 to 6 one trial at a time. The Chancellor doesn't do trials. It's one errand, and every
        step hands the next one what it needs: a password from an API, two people signed in at once, a tab that opens
        from a tab, a seal that has to be carried, and a file that goes out and comes back. That's what real end-to-end
        tests look like. This scroll is about keeping one long test, across two contexts, under control.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Know which page you're holding</h2>
    <p>
        With two contexts and a popup, a test holds several pages at once. Give each one a name that says who it
        belongs to, and never reach for <code>Page</code> out of habit. Events like popups and downloads fire on the page
        that caused them, so wait on that page:
    </p>
<pre><code>var clerk = Page;                                         // the test's own context

await using var chancellorContext = await Browser.NewContextAsync();
var chambers = await chancellorContext.NewPageAsync();    // a second cookie jar

var strongroom = await chambers.RunAndWaitForPopupAsync(() =&gt;
    chambers.GetByRole(AriaRole.Link, new() { Name = "Open the strongroom" }).ClickAsync());</code></pre>
    <p>The strongroom opened from the chambers, so it lives in the Chancellor's context and wears the Chancellor's cookies.</p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>The API is a step, too</h2>
    <p>
        <code>Page.APIRequest</code> belongs to a context, and shares its cookies. When an API call only fetches a value,
        like a password, it doesn't matter which context sends it. When it signs someone in, it matters a lot. Pull the
        value out, check the status as you go, and pass it on:
    </p>
<pre><code>var filed = await Page.APIRequest.PostAsync(registryUrl, new()
{
    DataObject = new { office = "Chancellor" },
    Headers = new Dictionary&lt;string, string&gt; { ["X-Petition"] = petition },
});
Assert.That(filed.Status, Is.EqualTo(201));
var record = await Page.APIRequest.GetAsync(new Uri(new Uri(Page.Url), filed.Headers["location"]).ToString(), new() { Headers = ... });
var password = (await record.JsonAsync())?.GetProperty("password").GetString();</code></pre>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Drag with intent</h2>
    <p>
        On floor 9 you dragged by hand with the mouse. When you only need to carry one thing onto another,
        <code>DragToAsync</code> does the pressing, moving and letting go for you:
    </p>
<pre><code>await strongroom.GetByRole(AriaRole.Img, new() { Name = "The Chancellor's seal" })
    .DragToAsync(strongroom.GetByRole(AriaRole.Region, new() { Name = "The charter" }));</code></pre>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Name the steps</h2>
    <p>
        A test this long reads best as a list of steps that return what the next step needs. Small private helpers do
        it, each taking the page it works on:
    </p>
<pre><code>var password = await AskTheRegistryAsync(petition);
await SignInAsync(clerk, "Clerk", clerkPassword);
var strongroom = await OpenTheStrongroomAsync(chambers, password);
var charter = await SealAndCollectAsync(strongroom);
await FileAsync(clerk, charter);</code></pre>
    <p>When it fails, the trace (from floor 7) shows the step. Start tracing on the second context too: each context keeps its own trace.</p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Face the Chancellor</h2>
    <p>
        A petition, two seals, a strongroom, a charter. Both people have to stay signed in until the end: a Chancellor who
        signs in again isn't the one who sealed it. Thirty seconds.
    </p>
</section>
